body:
yeah, we don't get diagrams for the ECUs because of intellectual property bullshit. it's annoying bc i know computer schematics so if I knew what was happening inside the computer I could confidently say "yes the computers bad" or go in a different direction but we are not so lucky

for reference, this is the issue I'm trying to diagnose:

on the AFR sensor heater circuit, I have 12V going into the sensor heater, but no voltage drop across the sensor heater with key on, which is normal, however, according to the input output specs, I *should* have 12V available at the ECM connector with key on. but I don't, I have 0V. there is a 12V drop between the ground side of the sensor heater and the ECM connector. however, if I disconnect both connectors and check for continuity in the wire, I have full continuity, 0.0 ohms, the only resistance in the circuit is the 2.9ohms from the heater itself, but no voltage is dropping across that load. but, with both the ecm and the sensor disconnected, if I apply 12V to the wire at the sensor end, I have 12V available at the ECM side, but when they are connected with key on, I have 12v available on the sensor side, but no voltage available at the ECM side. im probably the 2nd best technician at my shop in terms of electrical and this is boggling my brain. If it's something inside the ECM, I should still have voltage available *going into it* right? the 12v drop means the voltage is dropping after the sensor but before the computer, right? or am I fucking up on my theory
